caution because it's hard to know whether a&nbsp;
snowball sampling approach in these different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:56.520 --> 00:36:01.260
countries is what uh accounted for some of these&nbsp;
differences you know where you know one person&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:01.260 --> 00:36:05.280
might have shared it to their Network and then&nbsp;
that network is more homogeneous in a particular&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:05.280 --> 00:36:12.900
Global region but nonetheless we did find&nbsp;
overall that uh individuals from North America&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:12.900 --> 00:36:18.720
and Europe and Central Asia reported higher&nbsp;
levels of somatic symptoms severity relative&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:18.720 --> 00:36:23.400
to those in Latin America and the Caribbean&nbsp;
and relative to those in sub-Saharan Africa

00:36:25.620 --> 00:36:29.040
we found that same effect for Effective symptom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:29.040 --> 00:36:32.040
severity higher rates in Europe&nbsp;
and Central Asia and North America

00:36:34.620 --> 00:36:39.840
and then we found the same effect for cognitive&nbsp;
symptoms severity again highest in North America&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:39.840 --> 00:36:44.400
followed by Europe and Central Asia and&nbsp;
then sub-Saharan Africa and Latin America

00:36:49.380 --> 00:36:53.340
so again you know you're&nbsp;
seeing um kind of traditional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:53.940 --> 00:37:00.120
um psychology Behavioral Science approaches&nbsp;
with anovas and regression modeling so we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:00.120 --> 00:37:06.660
were interested in applying more modern data&nbsp;
science approaches to our data so we decided&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:06.660 --> 00:37:12.780
to apply a social network analysis to the 650&nbsp;
Spanish speakers with long coveted and then a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:12.780 --> 00:37:19.620
separate sample of 443 individuals without&nbsp;
a covid-19 history so the purpose of social&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:19.620 --> 00:37:26.160
network analysis is to model among other things&nbsp;
clusters of related symptoms as a dynamic system

00:37:28.380 --> 00:37:32.160
so in order to conduct this analysis we&nbsp;
use the estimate Network function from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:32.160 --> 00:37:37.140
the bootnet package in R we use the Epic&nbsp;
glasso routine to estimate a network of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:37.140 --> 00:37:43.920
partial polychloric correlations which you can&nbsp;
think of as you know partial correlations or a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:43.920 --> 00:37:48.360
relationship among variables controlling&nbsp;
for all other relationships in the net&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:50.220 --> 00:37:54.600
we use regularization which is a machine&nbsp;
learning algorithm that repeatedly reduces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:54.600 --> 00:37:59.520
the smallest edges from the network to zero&nbsp;
and then iteratively re-examines model fit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:59.520 --> 00:38:06.540
and we compared the two networks so this the uh&nbsp;
650 individuals with the history of long covet or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:06.540 --> 00:38:13.320
a history of covid and the 443 individuals without&nbsp;
the cobit history we compared those two networks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:13.320 --> 00:38:19.080
statistically using a 500 aeration permutation&nbsp;
test from the network comparison test package in r

00:38:22.200 --> 00:38:25.740
all right so graphically here are&nbsp;
the two networks so over on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:25.740 --> 00:38:29.580
left we have the long covid network over&nbsp;
on the right we have the control Network&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:30.180 --> 00:38:35.100
so yellow nodes or yellow symptoms&nbsp;
are the somatosensory symptoms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:35.820 --> 00:38:42.660
the blue symptoms are the cognitive symptoms and&nbsp;
then the green ones are the effective symptoms

00:38:46.320 --> 00:38:52.560
so over on the the right is the long covet&nbsp;
Network well we can immediately see is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:52.560 --> 00:38:58.740
by comparison the control network is largely&nbsp;
disjointed relative to the long coveted net&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:59.460 --> 00:39:04.680
so the somata sensory symptoms kind&nbsp;
of form their own symptom cluster and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:04.680 --> 00:39:10.380
don't actually bridge to any of the other&nbsp;
symptoms so it's kind of a diffuse just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:10.380 --> 00:39:16.620
pattern of symptoms in individuals without a&nbsp;
history of covid and then in those with long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:16.620 --> 00:39:23.400
covid we're seeing actually kind of a organized&nbsp;
interconnected set of neurobehavioral symptoms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:25.080 --> 00:39:30.060
then when you look within the models one of&nbsp;
the biggest differences that we found was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:30.060 --> 00:39:38.040
relationship between the organize and headache&nbsp;
nodes so in the long covet group we find a pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:38.040 --> 00:39:45.360
big connection between those two so the thicker&nbsp;
the line the stronger the connection among those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:45.360 --> 00:39:51.180
those variables of those nodes and then we find a&nbsp;
very little connection between the organized and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:51.180 --> 00:39:59.040
head headache nodes in the control net so does&nbsp;
that mean that headaches are causing people to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:59.040 --> 00:40:04.200
have organizational problems or does it mean that&nbsp;
there might be some organic brain damage in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:04.200 --> 00:40:10.080
context of long covid that's creating headache&nbsp;
pain and then also creating organizational issue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:10.080 --> 00:40:15.660
it's hard to know these data are all correlational&nbsp;
but nonetheless we're seeing that those those&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:15.660 --> 00:40:20.100
two symptoms in particular are highly related to&nbsp;
each other particularly relative to control group

00:40:23.220 --> 00:40:26.220
all right so if we're going to draw some&nbsp;
conclusions we have to think about a few&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:26.220 --> 00:40:31.560
limitations so you know again we used an&nbsp;
online snowball sampling approach and so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:31.560 --> 00:40:35.940
there's definitely some non-representativeness&nbsp;
of these samples we're not in any way saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:35.940 --> 00:40:41.820
that this represents um you know&nbsp;
an entire global population clearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:41.820 --> 00:40:45.420
there are a lot of global regions that&nbsp;
were not well represented like in Asia&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:47.100 --> 00:40:55.560
um also the uh the study was administered&nbsp;
either in English or Spanish and as a result&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:55.560 --> 00:41:01.320
yeah everybody had to speak those two languages&nbsp;
so there's profound limitations in how far the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:01.320 --> 00:41:05.340
um of the data collection can actually&nbsp;
spread just based on linguistic limitations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:06.840 --> 00:41:11.280
and you know participants also might have recall&nbsp;
bias when they're comparing their current symptoms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:11.280 --> 00:41:19.200
to those either during or before an active&nbsp;
covet infection so um I I would argue that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:19.980 --> 00:41:27.000
very likely the uh the reporting of telepsychology&nbsp;
used by psychologists was going to be a lot more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:27.000 --> 00:41:32.100
accurate because it's a very kind of concrete&nbsp;
behavioral anchor where when you're thinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:32.100 --> 00:41:37.320
about covid symptoms you have people who have&nbsp;
more mental health issues or more distressed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:37.320 --> 00:41:44.880
might retroactively view symptoms that they had&nbsp;
before the pandemic as uh being kind of higher in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:44.880 --> 00:41:50.700
magnitude or um you know and so as a result there&nbsp;
is some response bias that you have to take into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:50.700 --> 00:41:56.340
account but nonetheless we're very consistently&nbsp;
finding that individuals with that history of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:56.340 --> 00:42:02.400
covid are reporting continued symptoms and much&nbsp;
higher symptoms than they had before the pandemic

00:42:04.500 --> 00:42:09.840
now in terms of implications um you know it's&nbsp;
just you know we're finding that just incredible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:10.500 --> 00:42:14.940
um you know consistency across you&nbsp;
know all three domains of reporting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:14.940 --> 00:42:19.320
these substantial continued symptoms um and&nbsp;
so you know we're also seeing evidence that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:19.320 --> 00:42:24.660
long covet is a coherent identifiable&nbsp;
pattern of neurobehavioral impairments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:24.660 --> 00:42:29.460
now you might find uh this a little bit depressing&nbsp;
uh because you said oh my gosh you know they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:29.460 --> 00:42:35.280
based on you know coveted rates so many people are&nbsp;
probably experiencing these issues and it is true&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:35.280 --> 00:42:40.620
there are a lot of neurobehavioral impairments out&nbsp;
there that people are experiencing and there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:40.620 --> 00:42:47.340
neuropsychological tests that are showing evidence&nbsp;
of that as well but what I will say is that there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:47.340 --> 00:42:52.020
are emerging cognitive Rehabilitation approaches&nbsp;
that have been found to be extremely effective&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:52.020 --> 00:42:56.940
at treating long-term neurobehavioral symptoms&nbsp;
so I have colleagues at University of Alabama&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:42:56.940 --> 00:43:02.640
Birmingham who have found great evidence&nbsp;
already for uh some of their cognitive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:02.640 --> 00:43:06.420
Rehabilitation approaches so you can think of&nbsp;
these as like almost you know stereotypically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:06.420 --> 00:43:13.800
like brain training games where they do different&nbsp;
types of cognitive exercises um that help people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:13.800 --> 00:43:19.380
kind of adjust and you know regain function from&nbsp;
neurological conditions so this has been applied&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:19.380 --> 00:43:24.600
to traumatic brain injury stroke and now we're&nbsp;
finding evidence that it applies pretty well to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:24.600 --> 00:43:30.420
to Long coven and so we're just arguing that those&nbsp;
type of Rehabilitation Services and particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:30.420 --> 00:43:35.280
cognitive rehab are critical for individuals&nbsp;
reporting elevated long-coveted symptoms

00:43:38.640 --> 00:43:44.760
now if I were to take kind of a step back and&nbsp;
obviously this is a data science colloquium series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:45.420 --> 00:43:50.280
and so you know here we've showed that a&nbsp;
diverse set of data science approaches for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:50.280 --> 00:43:56.580
modeling important features of the covid-19&nbsp;
pandemic can include really a heterogeneous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:43:56.580 --> 00:44:01.740
set of analyzes so things like analyzes of&nbsp;
variance linear regressions path analyzes and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:01.740 --> 00:44:06.840
network analyzes and I would argue that each&nbsp;
of these approaches can answer you know very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:06.840 --> 00:44:12.960
different research questions even using the same&nbsp;
data set so you know a lot of times researchers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:12.960 --> 00:44:17.760
will start off with just describing means and&nbsp;
standard deviations and looking at differences&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:17.760 --> 00:44:22.980
among groups on some variable of interest and&nbsp;
then maybe they you know work their way up a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:22.980 --> 00:44:27.780
little bit further to you know regression models&nbsp;
where they might think of certain predictors and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:27.780 --> 00:44:32.520
try to you know fit the best regression model&nbsp;
that they can to you know predicting some outcome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:33.240 --> 00:44:37.920
but even still I would argue the field still&nbsp;
needs to you know take those approaches and move&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:37.920 --> 00:44:44.580
forward so you know have uh theoretical models&nbsp;
that you can test using path analyzes or even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:44.580 --> 00:44:50.160
more kind of bottom-up exploratory approaches like&nbsp;
network analysis that don't necessarily presuppose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:51.120 --> 00:44:56.220
um a prior existing Theory but nonetheless&nbsp;
can be very complementary because they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:44:56.220 --> 00:45:02.100
data driven they're inductive rather than&nbsp;
deductive so you know it's really this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:02.100 --> 00:45:07.860
combination of traditional and more modern data&nbsp;
science approaches that you can be extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:45:07.860 --> 00:45:12.900
